      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Medway%2C_Massachusetts"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1869 every street in Medway was named, honoring locals such as Winthrop, Kelley, Oakland, and Main.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        7,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Medway%2C_Massachusetts"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Founded in 1949, the Medway Historical Society curates the town’s past through tours, lectures, and exhibitions.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        3,
        4,
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://museumsdatabase.com/museums/view/14984"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The society’s headquarters at 223 Main Street was once the Sanford Tavern, a 19th‑century historic building.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://museumsdatabase.com/museums/view/14984"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Medway once housed six cotton and two woolen mills, two cotton‑wadding factories, and a bell foundry.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://newenglandtowns.org/massachusetts/medway"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The Charles River and Chicken Brook powered Medway’s early industry, spawning cotton and paper mills, straw and boot factories, and cottage workshops.",
